Post-Surgery Recovery: How Nutrition Speeds Up Healing
Surgery is a major stress on your body. Your body must repair tissue, control inflammation, and restore function simultaneously. Good nutrition during recovery directly affects how well you heal. Nutrition is thus integral part of clinical care
Why Nutrition for Post Surgery Recovery Matters
Healing requires energy and raw materials.
Protein: Rebuilds tissue, supports immune function, preserves muscle, provides amino acids for wound repair.
Carbohydrates and fat: Provide energy for recovery.
Vitamins and minerals:
Vitamin C: builds collagen (holds wounds together)
Zinc: supports cell division and immunity
Vitamin A: repairs skin and tissue
Iron: carries oxygen through blood
B vitamins: support energy and cell function
The challenge: Surgery often makes eating harder. Pain, nausea, constipation, reduced appetite, and digestive changes reduce food intake exactly when needs are highest. A practical nutrition plan focuses on what you can safely eat and whether intake is adequate, and not just generic advice.
