Start With Assessment, Not Generic Diet
The best nutrition for post surgery recovery is individualized. Your healthcare team should assess:
Unintended weight loss
Appetite and eating patterns
Swallowing or chewing difficulty
Digestive symptoms
Medical conditions (diabetes, kidney disease, etc., where specific tailored diet and exercise for diabetes management or renal protocols must be maintained alongside recovery)

Why this matters: Malnutrition increases risk of poor surgical outcomes. Patients at nutritional risk need early dietitian involvement.
Clinical nutrition approaches recognize that post-operative nutrition support must be tailored to the individual and the procedure. Also the surgery type changes everything: Nutrition after minor orthopaedic surgery differs from major gastrointestinal or cancer surgery. Always follow your surgeons instructions on texture, fluid intake, supplements, and feeding route.
